<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanns Eisler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Johannes Eisler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanns Eisler was an Austrian and German composer, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a> and the first of his disciples to compose in the twelve-tone technique. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> from 1925 he supported the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and began a collaboration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> that lasted until Brecht's death, writing music for The Decision and The Mother and the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;. Banned by the Nazis, he went into exile and in 1938 settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where his scores for Hangmen Also Die! and None but the Lonely Heart were nominated for Oscars. Accused before the House Un-American Activities Committee, he was deported in 1948; in East Berlin he composed the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler.jpg" alt="Hanns Eisler" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. M. Stieglitz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eisler1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two musicians, Ottmar Gerster and <b>Hanns Eisler</b>, proposed settings, and Eisler's was chosen.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Einheitsfrontlied&quot; (United Front Song) is one of the best-known songs of the German labour movement, written by Bertolt Brecht and set to music by <b>Hanns Eisler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li><li><blockquote>The words were by Johann Esser, a miner, and Wolfgang Langhoff, an actor; the music was composed by Rudi Goguel and later adapted by <b>Hanns Eisler</b> and Ernst Busch, Eisler changing its rhythm into two-four time.</blockquote><a href="/songs/peat-bog-soldiers">Peat Bog Soldiers</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Solidaritätslied&quot; (Solidarity Song) is a revolutionary workers' song written between 1929 and 1931 by Bertolt Brecht and set to music by <b>Hanns Eisler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied</a></li></ul></section>
